%N A119690 a(n) = n! mod n*(n+1)/2.
%C A119690 All terms are even.
%C A119690 It appears that f(n)=(n!)^(2k+1) modulo n(n+1)/2 is n if n is one less than an odd prime, else f(n) is 0, for any integer k. See A175567 for related results involving an even power of n!. - _John W. Layman_, Jul 12 2010
%C A119690 Layman's conjecture above is true for every k >= 0. Proof: if n+1 is not an odd prime, then n(n+1)/2 divides n! (see A069268), so every positive power of n! is 0 modulo n(n+1)/2. If n+1 = p is an odd prime, write n(n+1)/2 = h*p with h = n/2, gcd(h,p) = 1. Then n! == 0 (mod h), and n! == -1 (mod p) by Wilson's theorem, so for odd j, (n!)^j == 0 (mod h) and == -1 (mod p). Since n = 2h == 0 (mod h) and n = p-1 == -1 (mod p), the Chinese remainder theorem gives (n!)^j == n (mod n(n+1)/2), and 0 <= n < n(n+1)/2. - _Scott M. Foltz_, Sep 24 2026
%F A119690 a(n) = n if n+1 is an odd prime, a(n) = 0 otherwise.
%p A119690 P:=proc(n) local i, j, k; j:=1; k:=0; for i from 1 by 1 to n do j:=j*i; k:=k+i; print(j mod k); od; end: P(100);
%o A119690 (Magma) [ Factorial(n) mod (n*(n+1) div 2): n in [1..100] ];
%o A119690 (Python)
%o A119690 from sympy import isprime
%o A119690 def A119690(n): return n if n>1 and isprime(n+1) else 0 # based on formula in comments _Chai Wah Wu_, Sep 25 2026
